## 3. Train: required implementation and acceptance criteria

Trace how the train is supposed to be created, revealed, reached, boarded, moved, and unloaded. Inspect relevant models, storage locations, spawning code, mission gates, coordinates, visibility, streaming, permissions, and errors. Determine whether the train is absent, misplaced, hidden, not spawned, or inaccessible through the normal progression. Explain the verified cause.

Repair or build the train and its supporting station/platform as necessary. All of the following are required:

- A recognizable, visually finished train with an exterior and accessible passenger interior appropriate to the agreed design. Include readable entrances, appropriate materials, windows, wheels/bogies or equivalent train detail, seating, interior lighting, and believable platform/track context. It must not be a collection of undecorated boxes.
- From the player's first visit to the lobby, clearly communicate where the train is and how to access it. If it is intentionally available only after starting a mission, explain that requirement in the actual game and guide the player to the correct mission interaction and boarding location.
- The correct train must reliably appear or become accessible when its mission conditions are met. A missing train must not leave the mission silently waiting forever; provide an appropriate recovery or retry path.
- The player must be able to walk to the platform, use the boarding interaction or doorway, enter the passenger space, and experience the intended ride. Doors, prompts, collisions, and departure timing must support this sequence.
- Implement an actual in-game train journey with the motion, scenery, sound, and arrival behavior required by the design. Technical approaches may vary, but an unexplained instant teleport or a loading label alone does not count as train travel.
- Keep the player correctly positioned during the ride. Test for falling through the train, being left behind, camera clipping, blocked exits, and premature departure. If movement is intentionally restricted during part of the ride, make that clear and restore controls reliably.
- Arrival must lead to a usable exit or intended transition and the next playable objective. Returning to or retrying a mission must not create duplicate trains, stale prompts, or broken boarding state.

Verify the complete boarding–travel–arrival sequence using normal player interactions. Record the exact steps to reach it and capture actual runtime views of the boarding location, occupied interior, and arrival. A train model appearing in the Explorer is not sufficient evidence.

